BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Teaching Writing with Integrity with Dr. Lily Abadal
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T121500
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T131500
DTSTAMP:20261028T162551Z
UID:dartmouth-83080@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=83080\nhttps://airtable.com/appKrGzSjUnG5V1d3/shr4whdP9BZCDgPb2?p
 refill_I%20am%20registering%20for%3A=Teaching%20Writing%20With%20Integrity
 %20with%20Dr.%20Lily%20Abadal&prefill_Ask%20dietary%20question%3F=yes&hide
 _Ask%20dietary%20question%3F=true\n\nDescription:\nIf you have ever assign
 ed any form of prose in your classes\, you know that AI has completely upe
 nded the norms of assessment. Most instructors have responded with one of 
 three approaches. Some have opted to employ AI detection software to sniff
  out phony student work. Others have replaced take-home essays with blue b
 ook exams where students demonstrate their knowledge in short form in less
  than two hours. Finally\, others have ditched the essay entirely and have
  instead opted for oral exams and video reflection assignments.\nThis talk
  suggests there is another\, better way to keep the formative benefits of 
 the essay without the worry that students will over-rely on AI: a scaffold
 ed\, in-class\, term-long paper. We will cover how to scaffold in-class ar
 gumentative writing with a view toward cultivating the patience\, humility
 \, and honesty that genuine inquiry requires.\nLunch will be provided.\nPl
 ease register\n!\nDr. Lily Abadal is an Assistant Professor of Instruction
  in the Judy Genshaft Honors College at the University of South Florida. S
 he specializes in normative ethics\, applied ethics\, moral psychology\, a
 nd philosophy of education. Her research and work sit at the intersection 
 of AI ethics and character formation and she explore all things through a 
 Neo-Aristotelian lens. Dr. Abadal applies her research and experience on h
 uman formation as a consultant at Partners in Mission\, helping schools di
 scern how to prudently integrate AI technology (in both operations and in 
 the classroom) without diminishing the goods that make life worth living.\
 nThis event is co-sponsored by the Dartmouth Center for the Advancement of
  Learning\, the Ethics Institute\, the Leslie Center for the Humanities\, 
 the Department of Government\, and Learning Design & Innovation.\n\nSpons
 or / organizer: Dartmouth Center for the Advancement of Learning (DCAL)\, 
 Ethics Institute\, Government Department\, Learning Design and Innovation\
 , Leslie Center for the Humanities\n\nAudience: Faculty\, Postdoc\, Staff\
 , Students-Graduate\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: explici
 t food-service wording\; food keyword near service context\n\nFood availab
 ility is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before attendin
 g.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261028T162551Z
LOCATION:DCAL (Baker 102)
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=83080
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tess Martin: Experimental Animations
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261112T133000
DTSTAMP:20261028T162551Z
UID:dartmouth-83098@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=83098\n\nDescription:\nTess Martin: Experimental Animations\nTess
  Martin is an award-winning filmmaker/visual artist based in Rotterdam\, T
 he Netherlands. Her work is informed by hand-made animation techniques and
  their potential to explore our vulnerability in relation to nature\, the 
 onslaught of time and the precariousness of memory. She brings her perspec
 tive as someone  who was raised between cultures to explore themes of bel
 onging\, identity\, and connection. Many of her projects exist simultaneou
 sly as films and installations\, often incorporating sculptural elements a
 nd individually experienced soundtracks to create intimate durational enco
 unters. She often works with tactile materials such as paper cutouts\, pai
 nt\, ink\, sand\, and found objects\, pushing the boundaries between anima
 tion and contemporary art.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Film & Media Studies De
 partment\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included
 : Free Food category\; explicit food-service wording\; food keyword near s
 ervice context in summary\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source
  listing\; verify details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261028T162551Z
LOCATION:Visual Arts Center Room 108
